Let $M=\bigoplus_{\mu\in P}M_\mu$ be a weight module over $U_q(\mathfrak g)$ in the sense of [[def-quantum-weight-module-and-integrability]] ([[def-integrable-kac-moody-module]] for the classical notion), and suppose $M_A\subseteq M$ is an $A$-submodule, with $A=\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$, that is stable under $U_A$ and spans $M$ over $\mathbb Q(q)$ (the standing hypothesis for all specializations below; the lattice $M_A$ is exhibited in [[thm-quantum-highest-weight-modules-deform-the-classical-weight-character]]).

Let $k=\mathbb Z$ be made an $A$-algebra by $q\mapsto1$, and put $\bar M:=M_A\otimes_Ak$, with $\bar m$ the image of $m\in M_A$ and $\bar K_h,\bar T_{i,c,t},\bar E_i,\bar F_i$ the operators on $\bar M$ induced by $K_h,T_{i,c,t},E_i,F_i\in U_A$. Then, for every weight vector $m\in M_\mu$:

(i) $K_hm=q^{\mu(h)}m$, hence $\bar K_h\bar m=\bar m$: every $\bar K_h$ is the identity operator on $\bar M$, so a fortiori no residual $K_h$ ambiguity survives in the specialized operators;

(ii) $T_{i,c,t}m=\prod_{s=1}^{t}\frac{q^{\mu(h_i)+c-s+1}-q^{-(\mu(h_i)+c-s+1)}}{q^{s}-q^{-s}}m$, hence $\bar T_{i,c,t}\bar m=\binom{\mu(h_i)+c}{t}\bar m$; in particular $\bar T_{i,0,1}$ acts by the simple-coroot label $\mu(h_i)$, the classical Cartan generator, and $\bar T_{i,c,t}$ acts by the classical divided power $\binom{h_i+c}{t}$;

(iii) consequently $[\bar E_i,\bar F_j]=\delta_{ij}\bar T_{i,0,1}$, $[\bar T_{i,0,1},\bar E_j]=a_{ij}\bar E_j$ and $[\bar T_{i,0,1},\bar F_j]=-a_{ij}\bar F_j$ on $\bar M$, so the specialized action of the integral form recovers the classical Cartan labels rather than a rational expression.

The hypothesis that $M_A$ is $U_A$-stable is essential: this lemma computes the specialization of the *operators carried by an integral lattice*, and is not a claim that one may substitute $q=1$ into an arbitrary element of the rational form $U_q(\mathfrak g)$.

Let $I=\{1\}$, $A=(2)$, so that $U_q(\mathfrak g)=U_q(\mathfrak{sl}_2)$ with generators $E,F,K^{\pm1}$, $q_1=q$, and let $m\in\mathbb Z$. The quantum Verma module $M(m)=M(\lambda)$ with $\lambda(h_1)=m$ has $\mathbb Q(q)$-basis $\{v_t:=F^tv_0\}_{t\ge0}$ with $v_0=v_\lambda$, weights $m-2t$, and actions
$$Kv_t=q^{m-2t}v_t,\qquad Fv_t=v_{t+1},\qquad Ev_t=[m-t+1]_qv_{t-1}\quad(t\ge1),\qquad Ev_0=0,$$
as follows from [[thm-quantized-sl-two-string-formulas]](iii) with $N=m$ when $m\ge0$ and by the same computation in general. In particular:

(a) if $m\ge0$, the submodule generated by $F^{m+1}v_0$ is $\bigoplus_{t\ge m+1}\mathbb Q(q)v_t$, the quotient $\tilde L(m)=M(m)/U_qF^{m+1}v_0$ is the $(m+1)$-dimensional simple module with basis $v_0,\dots,v_m$, $Kv_t=q^{m-2t}v_t$, $Fv_t=[t+1]_qv_{t+1}$, $Ev_t=[m-t+1]_qv_{t-1}$, and for $m=0$ this is the one-dimensional trivial module;

(b) if $m=-1$, then $[m-t+1]_q=[-t]_q=-[t]_q\ne0$ for all $t\ge1$, so no nonzero vector of positive weight-string index is killed by $E$ and $M(-1)$ is irreducible of infinite dimension: the simple module $L(-1)$ is not integrable, and the highest weight is not dominant integral;

(c) for every $m<0$ the string coefficient $[m-t+1]_q$ vanishes only at the nonpositive integer $t=m+1\le0$, so as in (b) the Verma module $M(m)$ is irreducible, infinite-dimensional and not integrable; the dominance hypothesis $m\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}$ in [[thm-integrable-quantum-highest-weight-module-exists-and-is-unique]] cannot be relaxed.
